sections.body:
`@Value` injects a single resolved property directly into a field, constructor parameter, or method parameter, using a `${...}` placeholder that Spring resolves against the `Environment`:

```java
@Component
public class NotificationClient {

    @Value("${app.notifications.retry-count:3}")
    private int retryCount;

    @Value("${app.notifications.channel}")
    private String channel;
}
```

`${app.notifications.retry-count:3}` resolves `app.notifications.retry-count` through the Environment, using `3` as a fallback if that key isn't defined anywhere across any registered property source. `${app.notifications.channel}`, with no default supplied, behaves differently in a way worth knowing precisely: if that key is missing from every property source at the point Spring tries to resolve it, `@Value` doesn't silently inject `null` -- it throws `IllegalArgumentException: Could not resolve placeholder 'app.notifications.channel'` at application startup, which is a genuinely useful fail-fast behavior for a required property with no sensible default.

The practical weakness of `@Value` shows up specifically when a default IS supplied. `@Value("${app.notifications.channel:email}")` will happily, silently fall back to `"email"` forever if the real property key is ever misspelled anywhere in configuration -- there is no missing-property error to notice, because as far as Spring is concerned, nothing is actually missing; the default mechanism is working exactly as designed. The bug hides in plain sight: someone intended to configure `app.notifications.channel=sms` in a profile-specific file, typo'd it as `app.notification.channel` (singular), and the application starts up cleanly, always uses the default, and gives no signal anywhere that the real intended override was never applied.

`@ConfigurationProperties` takes a different approach: rather than pulling in properties one at a time, it binds every property under a given prefix onto the fields of one POJO in a single pass:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
public class NotificationProperties {
    private int retryCount = 3;
    private String channel;
    // getters and setters
}
```

This alone is not enough to make binding actually happen -- `@ConfigurationProperties` is metadata describing a *binding target*, not a stereotype annotation that registers a bean the way `@Component` does. The class needs to become a Spring bean through one of a few routes: annotate it `@Component` as well (making it discoverable via ordinary component scanning), register it explicitly with `@EnableConfigurationProperties(NotificationProperties.class)` on a `@Configuration` class, or -- the most common modern approach -- add `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` to the main application class, which scans for and registers every `@ConfigurationProperties`-annotated class it finds, the same way `@ComponentScan` does for stereotype-annotated classes. Forgetting this registration step is a genuinely common mistake: the class compiles fine, looks completely correct, and yet every field silently stays at whatever default you hardcoded in the class itself, because nothing in the container ever actually attempted to bind real configuration values onto it.

Rather than binding via setters onto a mutable object with default field values, `@ConfigurationProperties` supports **constructor binding** -- populating a class's fields entirely through its constructor:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
public class NotificationProperties {

    private final int retryCount;
    private final String channel;

    public NotificationProperties(int retryCount, String channel) {
        this.retryCount = retryCount;
        this.channel = channel;
    }
    // getters only -- no setters, no default field values
}
```

When a class has exactly one constructor, Spring Boot uses it automatically for constructor binding (older Spring Boot versions required an explicit `@ConstructorBinding` annotation on the constructor; a single constructor is enough as of Spring Boot 3). The payoff mirrors the exact same argument the Core Concepts module made for preferring constructor injection over field injection on ordinary beans: fields can be declared `final`, guaranteeing they're set exactly once and never mutated after construction, and the object genuinely cannot exist in a half-configured state -- every field is populated as part of one atomic construction step, rather than a mutable object that starts out at its Java-level defaults and gets setters called on it afterward, potentially incompletely, in whatever order the binder happens to process keys.

`@ConfigurationProperties` classes can be annotated `@Validated` and use the exact same JSR-380 constraint annotations (`@NotBlank`, `@Min`, `@Pattern`, and others) already familiar from validating request DTOs in the Building REST APIs module:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
@Validated
public class NotificationProperties {

    @NotBlank
    private final String channel;

    @Min(0)
    private final int retryCount;

    public NotificationProperties(@NotBlank String channel, @Min(0) int retryCount) {
        this.channel = channel;
        this.retryCount = retryCount;
    }
}
```

Spring Boot validates the fully-bound object against these constraints at application startup, and refuses to start at all if any constraint fails -- the exact same fail-fast philosophy Bean Validation gave incoming request bodies, now applied to configuration itself. This is a real, structural advantage over `@Value`: there is no equivalent built-in validation layer for `@Value`-injected fields at all -- if you want to check that a `@Value`-injected number is non-negative, you write that check by hand, in code, after injection, and it only runs if and when something actually exercises that code path. A validated `@ConfigurationProperties` class catches a misconfigured deployment the moment it starts, before it ever serves a single request, which is precisely the difference between finding a configuration mistake in a startup log and finding it three hours into a production incident.

examples.code:
@Component
public class NotificationClient {

    @Value("${app.notifications.retry-count:3}")
    private int retryCount;

    @Value("${app.notifications.channel}")
    private String channel;   // no default -- REQUIRED
}

// If app.notifications.channel is missing from every property source:
// IllegalArgumentException: Could not resolve placeholder 'app.notifications.channel'
// -- thrown at application startup, before the bean is even fully constructed.

@SpringBootApplication
@ConfigurationPropertiesScan
public class DemoApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}

@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
public class NotificationProperties {
    private int retryCount = 3;
    private String channel = "email";

    public int getRetryCount() { return retryCount; }
    public void setRetryCount(int retryCount) { this.retryCount = retryCount; }
    public String getChannel() { return channel; }
    public void setChannel(String channel) { this.channel = channel; }
}

@Service
public class NotificationService {
    private final NotificationProperties properties;

    public NotificationService(NotificationProperties properties) {
        this.properties = properties;
    }
}

@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
@Validated
public class NotificationProperties {

    @NotBlank
    private final String channel;

    @Min(0)
    private final int retryCount;

    public NotificationProperties(String channel, int retryCount) {
        this.channel = channel;
        this.retryCount = retryCount;
    }
    // getters only
}

// application.properties:
// app.notifications.channel=
// app.notifications.retry-count=-1
//
// Application fails to start:
// Binding validation errors for prefix 'app.notifications'
//   - Field 'channel': must not be blank
//   - Field 'retryCount': must be greater than or equal to 0

