sections.body:
`@Profile` conditionally includes or excludes a `@Bean` or `@Component`-annotated class from the application context, based on which profiles are currently active:

```java
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String recipient, String message);
}

@Component
@Profile("dev")
public class LoggingNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        System.out.println("[DEV] Would notify " + recipient + ": " + message);
    }
}

@Component
@Profile("prod")
public class SesNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        // ... real AWS SES call ...
    }
}
```

This is exactly the `Notifier`/`EmailNotifier` interface-based design from the Core Concepts module's Inversion of Control example, extended one step further: instead of only one implementation being registered, two are declared, and `@Profile` decides which one is actually eligible for a given run -- with the injecting code (whatever depends on `Notifier`) never needing to know or care which concrete implementation it received. Running with `dev` active registers only `LoggingNotifier`; running with `prod` active registers only `SesNotifier`. If the active profile matches NEITHER bean's `@Profile` value, the application fails to start with `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException` the moment something needs a `Notifier` -- the same fail-fast unresolved-dependency behavior already familiar from ordinary IoC. And if a deployment mistakenly activates a profile combination where BOTH beans end up eligible at once (covered next), the application fails to start with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` instead -- ambiguous injection, for exactly the same reason two unqualified `@Component` beans implementing the same interface would conflict with no profiles involved at all.

